// Regression test: a `for` loop ranging over doubles must correctly infer
// its loop variable as `double` and allow the `step` clause to use it as
// such. This is actually two bugs fixed together, both discovered and
// root-caused while reviewing Resolver.cpp/TypeChecker.cpp (this file used
// to live in tests/10_known_bugs/ as fail_double_for_loop_step.tr, tracking
// the first one before the second was found):
//
//  1. TypeChecker::visitFor() used to visit the `step` expression BEFORE
//     populating its LoopVarTypes_ map with the range's correctly-inferred
//     type -- so `step x += 1.0` saw the loop variable typed as `int`
//     (Resolver::visitFor()'s unconditional default) and failed to compile
//     with "Cannot assign value of type 'double' to variable of type 'int'".
//
//  2. Fixing #1 alone made the loop COMPILE but then HANG FOREVER at
//     runtime: TypeChecker's LoopVarTypes_ is private, type-checking-only
//     bookkeeping that Codegen's getExprType() never consults -- it reads
//     ResolvedSymbol->Type directly, which was still the Resolver's stale
//     `int`. CompoundAssignmentExprAST::codegen() trusted that wrong answer
//     and emitted a raw bitcast-to-i64 / add-1 / bitcast-back instead of
//     CreateFAdd, incrementing the double's raw BIT PATTERN by 1 (an
//     ULP-scale change) instead of its VALUE by 1.0 -- so the loop condition
//     was never going to become false in any practical number of
//     iterations. Fixed by having visitFor() also commit the inferred type
//     back onto Node.ResolvedLoopVar (the actual shared VariableSymbol every
//     reference to the loop variable resolves to), mirroring how
//     visitVarDecl() already corrects ResolvedSymbol->Type when resolving a
//     NamedType to its real struct type.

fn main(): int {
    mut sum: double = 0.0;
    for x in 0.0..3.0 step x += 1.0 {
        sum = sum + x;
    }
    assert(sum > 2.9 && sum < 3.1, "");
    return 0;
}
